O.k.  A few more calculations.  Same formula, different probabilities.
I take all potions of *healing, having read that their total generation
probability is 11%.  Instead of levels, I will just speak of potions
found so far.  Clearly, when talking about average number of potions per
level, one has to specify the sample considered.  (This was only
implicit in my last post.)  One could be preposterous and take all games
that have been played and will be played, ever.  The disadvantage is,
nobody can know that number (although decent estimates can be had).
Obviously, extinctionist games should be thrown out of the sample, they
are "atypical".  The sample I had in mind is the set of regular,
no-conduct initial games without any attempt at high speed, i.e. usual
exploration intensity, (almost) completing each level (unless rooms are
not found).  For this, I am pretty sure that one can say that the
average number of potions found per level is bigger than one, because I
usually find more than 25 potions on the 25 first levels and smaller
than 10, because I don't think I ever found as many as 250 on this
number of levels.  Therefore, I took 4 as an estimate.  But this will
not be of importance in the following.

So assume we have found 100 potions so far (no matter what level). What
is the probability that there are no more than 10 potions of *healing
among these?  It turns out that this is 45%, pretty close to the 44%
that I got for 5 potions of healing.  (I had a reason to choose such a
small number :-) ).  If we ask for the probability of not having more
than 15 potions, this is 92%.  So *if* the number of potions found per
level is 4 or 5, you are not going to get 15 potions of *healing on the
first 20 to 25 levels (including mines) in 9 out of 10 games.

Now let us go to 150 potions found.  Then the probability of having
found no more than 10 potions of *healing drops to 5%, while the
probability of having found 15 or fewer is still 40%.

At 200 potions found, the probability that not even ten of them are of
the healing type is only 0.23%, so this will happen in 2 out of 1000
games only.  So, *if* the average number of potions per level were
indeed 4, at the dungeon bottom you should have enough *healing potions
(supposing none were spent) to go up by 80 HP. For 15 potions, the odds
of not having found at least that number are still 6.5%, so in six to
seven games out of hundred one may not be able to get 120 HP from
*healing potions after having found 200 potions in total.  (Supposing
again, one does not selectively find only those potions that are not
used up by monsters.)

